<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2600–2000 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sacred City of Caral-Supe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caral is an archaeological site in the Supe valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, about 23 km from the coast, and the largest centre of the Norte Chico or Caral-Supe civilisation. Its platform mounds, sunken circular plazas and houses date from about 2600 BCE, making it one of the oldest cities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Excavated from 1994 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruth-shady">Ruth Shady</a>, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caral.jpg" alt="Caral" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jon Gudorf Photography · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mayor_Pyramid_and_circular_enclosure._Caral,_Peru.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Caral lies in the Supe District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, near the present town of Caral, 182 kilometres (113 mi) north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, 23 kilometres (14 mi) from the coast and 350 metres above sea level. It is the best studied and one of the largest sites of the Norte Chico civilisation, with a population of around 3,000. Nineteen other temple complexes were scattered across the 90 square kilometres (35 sq mi) of the Supe Valley, and the area may have held 20,000 people sharing the same culture.</p><p>Closely related was an early fishing city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspero">Áspero</a>, on the coast near the mouth of the Supe River. The date of 2627 BCE for Caral rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> of reeds and woven carrying bags used to haul stone for the temples. Excavators describe it as the oldest urban centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, a claim challenged by the discovery of other ancient sites nearby, such as Bandurria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-uhle">Max Uhle</a> found Caral in 1905 during a survey of ancient Peruvian cities but did not recognise the hills as pyramids. Paul Kosok studied it more fully in 1948, and others doubted the age he claimed. In 1975 the architect Carlos Williams recorded most sites of the Supe valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruth-shady">Ruth Shady</a> then explored the city, which was inhabited from about 2600 BCE to 2000 BCE.</p><p>The city had two halves divided by the Supe River valley. The Upper Half held six monumental complexes, each with a pyramid, open plaza and houses, where food remains such as sea lion bones point to elites. The Lower Half had smaller houses, with diets of mostly crops and some fish, and one complex, the Temple of the Amphitheater. The present explanation is that the city was planned this way, so that the layout expressed political power and social inequality. The main temple is 150 metres long, 110 metres wide and 28 metres high.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Shady found no sign of warfare at Caral, such as battlements, weapons or mutilated bodies. One temple held 32 flutes made of condor and pelican bones, and a collection of 37 cornetts of deer and llama bone and 33 flutes was also found. Excavators labelled a knotted textile a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipu</a>, which they take as evidence that this record-keeping system was older than thought. A geoglyph of a human face lies west of the site.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared the city a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2009. Scholars believe that research at Caral may answer questions about the origins of Andean civilisations, since its urban design seems to have been a model for later ones.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Caral</b>–Supe builders hauled stones to their pyramids in loosely meshed bags of a highland grass, shicra, packing the filled bags behind each retaining wall; radiocarbon dates of these bags date Caral to about 2627 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/caral-supe-civilisation">Caral–Supe civilisation · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Caral</b>, about 23 km inland in the Supe valley, became the largest of some twenty related sites.</blockquote><a href="/state/caral-supe">Caral-Supe</a></li></ul></section>
